<section class="tale-chapter" id="narada-question"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>The question that begins the tale</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.33–41 · supplied PDF p. 1632</p><p>Narayana had been speaking to Narada about the power of the Divine Mother and the succession of the Manus. He had named Bhramari Devi among the manifestations of the Goddess. Narada, eager to understand that extraordinary name, interrupted with a question. Who was Bhramari? Why had the Mother of the universe appeared in this form, and what had taken place when she did?</p><p>The answer becomes a tale of ambition, a difficult boon, and the strange limits of worldly power. It also carries a description of the Goddess unlike the more familiar images of battle with sword or spear. In this telling, the creatures associated with her are bees, and the force that overcomes an army comes from their countless numbers.</p><p>Narayana begins by recalling a powerful Daitya named Aruna. Aruna desired mastery over the Devas, and he resolved to obtain an advantage no opponent could readily break.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="arunas-penance"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>Aruna's austerities beside the Ganga</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.33–49 · supplied PDF pp. 1632–1633</p><p>Aruna withdrew to the banks of the Ganga in the Himalayas and undertook an extreme discipline directed toward Brahma. The account describes long successive periods during which he survived on fallen leaves, then only water, then air, and finally on no ordinary nourishment. He repeated the Gayatri mantra as his austerities grew more severe.</p><p>Thousands of years pass within the story's Puranic scale. Aruna's body becomes wasted by his penance, yet the heat of his tapas spreads outward with such force that the worlds are disturbed. The Devas, alarmed by the blazing energy, turn to Brahma.</p><p>Brahma comes to the place of penance accompanied by Gayatri. He appears on his swan, and Aruna opens his eyes to behold the creator before him. What Aruna has gained through endurance is now the opportunity to ask for a boon. The question is what he will ask, and what that wish will make of him.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="brahmas-boon"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>A boon with a hidden boundary</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.50–59 · supplied PDF p. 1633</p><p>Aruna first asks that death should never come to him. Brahma refuses. Immortality in that absolute form, he explains, cannot be bestowed in the way the Daitya demands. Aruna then changes his request. If he cannot be exempt from death altogether, he wants to be protected from death in battle, from ordinary weapons, and from men and women. He further asks to be safe from two-footed and four-footed beings, and from their combinations.</p><p>Brahma grants the requested protection. The language of the boon is decisive: Aruna has named the kinds of danger he thinks he needs to escape, but the world contains other possibilities. The narrative does not present his protection as limitless, even though Aruna treats it as an assurance that no rival can prevail.</p><p>With the boon in hand, Aruna summons the other Daityas and directs his followers toward an assault on the heavens. Their messengers carry a challenge to the Devas.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/bhramari-aruna-boon.png" alt="In an imagined Himalayan riverside scene, four-faced Brahma appears on his swan before the male Daitya Aruna after his penance" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"><figcaption>ARUNA RECEIVES THE BOON</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="conquest"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>The heavens fall into Aruna's hands</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.59–70 · supplied PDF pp. 1633–1634</p><p>The message of invasion reaches Indra, and the Devas are shaken. They seek counsel from Brahma, then from Vishnu, and finally gather in Shiva's realm to consider how Aruna might be opposed. While they deliberate, the Daitya marches into heaven with his forces.</p><p>Aruna's accumulated power and his newly granted protection transform the balance between the sides. He drives the Devas from their stations and takes over the functions or possessions associated with celestial powers such as the Sun, Moon, Yama and Agni. Those who once occupied the heavens are themselves displaced.</p><p>The Devas cannot find a straightforward answer. Weapons and conventional opponents are precisely what the boon seems to exclude. Their predicament forces them to look beyond the battlefield and ask where protection truly comes from.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="heavenly-counsel"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>The counsel of an unseen voice</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.60–70 · supplied PDF p. 1634</p><p>As the Devas remain unable to devise a successful attack, a disembodied heavenly voice announces a different course. They are to worship the Queen of the Universe. It also tells them that if Aruna ceases his repetition of Gayatri, the protection he draws from that practice will no longer sustain him in the same way.</p><p>The plan therefore has two parts. The Devas must direct their devotion toward the Divine Mother, and Aruna must be led to abandon the very discipline through which he had risen. It is a striking turn: the strength acquired in austerity has become entangled with pride and conquest.</p><p>Indra asks Brihaspati, the preceptor of the Devas, to approach Aruna. The Devas themselves prepare to worship the Goddess and seek her intervention.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="brihaspati"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>Brihaspati approaches the Daitya</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.71–78 · supplied PDF pp. 1634–1635</p><p>Brihaspati goes to Aruna in the appearance of a sage. The Daitya asks why one of the Devas' party has come into the presence of their enemy. Brihaspati replies by pointing to their shared reverence for Gayatri: if they worship the same divine power, why must Aruna insist that he stands apart from them?</p><p>The passage tells of Aruna becoming confused by the exchange. In arrogance and delusion, he ceases the Gayatri practice that had accompanied his penance. The inner fire supporting his strength fades. Brihaspati then returns and informs the Devas that his mission has succeeded.</p><p>This episode should not be turned into an independent instruction about mantra practice. It belongs to the story's own causal sequence, in which Aruna's increasing pride brings about a loss of the steadiness on which he had relied.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="devas-pray"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>The Devas turn toward the Mother</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.71–85 · supplied PDF pp. 1634–1635</p><p>While Brihaspati undertakes his mission, the Devas perform worship of the Divine Mother. The text describes their offerings, mantra recitation and severe devotion. Their plea is not merely for a stronger weapon; they seek the presence of the one whom the heavenly voice has named as their refuge.</p><p>After a time the Goddess appears before them. Narayana describes a radiance of extraordinary brilliance and beauty, garlands and ornaments, and a majestic form attended by innumerable dark bees. The gathering is transformed by her arrival.</p><p>The Devas, who could neither regain heaven by their own power nor find an answer in their council, behold the Goddess. In the story, their help comes through a manifestation beyond the kinds of beings and weapons that Aruna had thought to exclude.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="bhramari-appears"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>Bhramari Devi, encircled by bees</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.78–85 · supplied PDF p. 1635</p><p>The description of Bhramari Devi is central to this account. She is adorned with fine garments, ornaments and remarkable garlands. Great black bees gather around her neck and move in vast numbers about her form. The text speaks of bees held within her hands and of gestures that bestow boons and freedom from fear.</p><p>Their sound fills the scene. This is why the name Bhramari matters in the tale: the bees are not a small decorative detail added to a familiar image of the Goddess. They are the distinctive sign of the manifestation through which she will restore the Devas.</p><p>The image of the Goddess is both compassionate and formidable. Those who seek shelter bow before her; those who have seized the heavens are about to face a force not covered by their king's boastful expectations.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="praise"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>09</span><h2>The praise of the Divine Mother</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.86–109 · supplied PDF pp. 1635–1636</p><p>Brahma and the other Devas praise the Goddess as the source and support of creation, preservation and dissolution. Their hymn speaks of knowledge, of divine power in its many forms, and of the Mother as the refuge of beings. It invokes a range of her names and manifestations rather than treating Bhramari as a power separate from the supreme Goddess.</p><p>They particularly acknowledge the black bees encircling her. After their worship, the Goddess asks what they desire. The Devas explain Aruna's conquest, the loss of their own stations and the protection granted through Brahma's boon.</p><p>The Goddess hears the petition. The answer follows not as a contest between armies of comparable strength, but as an overwhelming release of the living creatures gathered about her.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="divine-bees"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>10</span><h2>When the sky darkened with bees</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.109–120 · supplied PDF pp. 1636–1637</p><p>From the Goddess come vast numbers of black bees, hornets and similar creatures. They spread in every direction, joining the swarms already issuing from her hands. The sky grows dark; mountain slopes, forests and open land are covered by their movement. The narrative likens their abundance to a world-filling multitude.</p><p>The Daityas are unable to meet the assault with their weapons or to organize a defense. The creatures descend upon their forces, and the armies that had made the heavens their possession are overcome. This is the strange opening left by Aruna's boon: the means of defeat are neither the usual weapon nor the kind of opponent against which he had sought security.</p><p>When the Daityas have fallen, the bees return to the Goddess. The sudden quiet after their immense movement gives way to amazement among those who have witnessed what occurred. The divine mother has undone the seemingly invincible power of Aruna without repeating the familiar form of armed combat.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/bhramari-divine-swarm.png" alt="A dramatic artistic interpretation of countless dark bees overtaking Aruna's army beneath the radiant presence of Bhramari Devi" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE SKY FILLS WITH DIVINE BEES</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="blessing"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>11</span><h2>The return of praise and peace</h2></div><p class="collection-source">Book 10, Chapter 13.121–127 · supplied PDF pp. 1637–1638</p><p>The Devas and sages rejoice. Flowers are offered, hymns are sung, instruments sound, and the atmosphere that had been dominated by fear becomes one of thanksgiving. Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva join the praise of the Divine Mother.</p><p>The Goddess grants boons to the assembled Devas. They seek steadfast devotion to her, and she gives that blessing before withdrawing from their sight. Narayana then tells Narada of the spiritual merit associated in the text with hearing the account of Bhramari Devi.</p><p>The story has moved from a Daitya's pursuit of invulnerability to the Devas' acknowledgment of a power beyond their own. Aruna tried to secure himself by defining every possible adversary. Bhramari's appearance shows how incomplete those calculations were. The final emphasis, however, rests on the Mother's protection and the gratitude of those who turned toward her.</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>What can a boon<br><em>never guarantee?</em></h2><p>Aruna tries to make himself unassailable by naming every opponent he imagines. The story asks how pride can narrow a person's understanding of danger, and how the Devas' appeal to the Divine Mother differs from Aruna's pursuit of domination.</p><ul><li>Why does Aruna's great discipline fail to protect him from his own arrogance?</li><li>What does the form of Bhramari Devi reveal about the limits of force and weapons?</li><li>Why does the story end with the Devas seeking lasting devotion rather than another advantage?</li></ul><p>These are editorial questions, not quoted statements from the Purana.</p></section>
<section class="tale-tradition" id="traditions"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>One named manifestation.<br><em>One particular textual account.</em></h2><p><strong>Primary supplied text:</strong> <cite>Srimad Devi Bhagavata Purana</cite>, English translation attributed to Swami Vijnananda, Book 10, Chapter 13, especially verses 33–127, PDF pp. 1632–1638. Verses 1–32 conclude the preceding account of the Manus and set up Narada's question. The story is an original English prose retelling, not a verbatim translation or ritual guide.</p><p>The Purana's narrative includes Aruna's Gayatri recitation, Brihaspati's intervention and the appearance of innumerable bees. It should not be silently combined with different stories of the Goddess found in the Devi Mahatmya or other Puranas. The four images used across the site are separate cinematic artistic interpretations; the text does not identify historical faces, actual locations or a fixed colour for the Goddess's garments, and ornamental details in the artwork are not presented as scripturally prescribed iconography.</p><a href="/sources.html#bhramari-aruna">Read the source-by-passage map and editorial notes</a></section>
